  "verified_against": {
    "source": "release-manifest.json",
    "agrees": true,
    "note": "Hashes were downloaded from the published v5.40.0 release assets and hashed independently, then checked again by re-fetching the URL this pin itself names. Only the pegasus artifact changed: install.sh and the two builder scripts are byte-identical to v5.28.0 and earlier. v5.40.0 reaches this distribution through the shared prompt and the tool-description plugin, so it changes how every agent behaves here under DARQ's own names. An agent asked to edit a file owned by root got a PermissionDenied from its editing tool, then read the shared prompt's rule -- if the runtime denies a tool, that denial stands, say which tool and stop -- and concluded every other route was closed too, including the shell with sudo, which it had been granted and which the person had asked for. That rule exists to stop an agent from routing around a permission decision the person made, and it is unchanged for that. What was missing is that the two are not the same thing: a tool the runtime denies is a decision about the agent and closes every path; a PermissionDenied returned BY a tool is a fact about the file -- that tool writes as the user the session runs as, and the file belongs to someone else -- and says nothing about what another tool can reach. The prompt now tells them apart, and extends to the privileged local file the symmetry it already had for the remote one: the shell is not a way around the editing tools, it is the way there, on the same condition the SSH case already carried -- when the person asked for that change. The same correction now also travels on the channel that outweighs the prompt: the scope note this distribution's own apply_patch plugin appends covers the privileged local file as well as the remote one, and starts on its own paragraph, which it did not before. Separately, assigning a model now writes itself into the rendered configuration the way granting an MCP server or a directory already did, so the second command that used to be required is gone; with no installation it refuses with the same message its siblings use, and the models screen no longer opens for a CLI with nothing installed. And the install flow's MCP selection screen stopped retiring bound servers -- one bound against a key the person administers is part of the installation, so it opens checked and shows its key, and continuing untouched reproduces the machine's state, which is what that screen claimed to do and did not. This distribution changed nothing of its own for this pin beyond the alignment."
  }
}
